"Script written. Let me verify it works by running it against a copy of the database, so `migration.db` itself stays untouched:Created `deadlock_sim.py` in the workspace. Verified with two runs against a scratch copy of the db (so `migration.db` balances stay untouched).\n\nHow it works:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[\"Thread A<br/>BEGIN → lock acct 1 → sleep\"] -->|tries UPDATE acct 2| C((database<br/>locked))\n    B[\"Thread B<br/>BEGIN → lock acct 2 → sleep\"] -->|tries UPDATE acct 1| C\n    C --> D[\"SQLite breaks the cycle:<br/>one side gets SQLITE_BUSY,<br/>other commits\"]\n```\n\n- Opens two separate connections (`timeout=5.0`, `check_same_thread=False`)\n- `threading.Barrier` releases both threads simultaneously to maximize contention\n- Thread A: reads + updates account 1, sleeps 0.1s, updates account 2\n- Thread B: same, but in reverse order (2 then 1), producing the lock-order inversion\n- Both ran in deferred transactions, so each holds SHARED and upgrades to RESERVED; the cycle makes the second writer fail with `database is locked`\n- Failures are caught, rolled back cleanly, and reported, with final balances printed\n- Optional CLI arg for db path; exits 1 only if both threads fail\n\nSample output: `A FAILED: database is locked` while `B committed` (balances 110 / 90 on the copy)."